1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,557
This is STAT 331, video 13 a. The topic is multiple linear regression: the model, and what its coefficients mean.

2
00:00:10,007 --> 00:00:16,450
This video prepares you for two question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:28,228
Type A: write the model in matrix form, with the vector y, the design matrix X, the vector beta, and their shapes. Tutorial 2, question 3 b, asks this.

4
00:00:28,678 --> 00:00:39,451
Type B: interpret beta 0 and beta j, holding the other covariates fixed. Video 13 b computes and derives the estimate of beta.

5
00:00:39,901 --> 00:00:58,013
Lecture 6 starts with a recap of three tools from Lecture 5. Video 13 b uses them. First, matrix calculus. z equals a transpose y. The picture shows the shapes: 1 by k times k by 1. So z is one number.

6
00:00:58,463 --> 00:01:05,269
The derivative of z with respect to the vector y is the vector a. The arrow in the picture shows it.

7
00:01:05,719 --> 00:01:15,084
For a quadratic form, y transpose A y, the derivative is A y plus A transpose y. If A is symmetric, this is 2 A y.

8
00:01:15,534 --> 00:01:30,403
Second, random vectors. For a constant matrix A, the expected value of A y is A times the expected value of y. The variance of A y is A, times the variance of y, times A transpose.

9
00:01:30,853 --> 00:01:43,717
Third, the multivariate normal. Take z with independent standard normal entries. Then A z plus mu is multivariate normal, with mean mu and variance A A transpose.

10
00:01:44,167 --> 00:01:55,218
A linear function C y plus d of an M V N vector is also M V N, with mean C mu plus d and variance C Sigma C transpose.

11
00:01:55,668 --> 00:02:12,116
Section 1. Here is the data example of Lecture 6. The file satisfaction dot csv has data on hospital patients. The response y is satisfaction with the quality of care. A higher value means more satisfied.

12
00:02:12,566 --> 00:02:23,211
There are three covariates. Age is in years. Severity is a score for the condition of the patient. Stress is the stress level that the patient reports.

13
00:02:23,661 --> 00:02:36,184
Each patient is one row. There are n equals 46 rows. The table shows the first 6 rows. We want to explain satisfaction with all three covariates at the same time.

14
00:02:36,634 --> 00:02:52,847
A pairs plot shows a scatter plot for each pair of variables. In row i and column j, the vertical axis is variable i and the horizontal axis is variable j. The diagonal gives the names and the ranges.

15
00:02:53,297 --> 00:03:11,985
The first row, outlined in orange, has satisfaction on the vertical axis. All three plots go down. The correlations are minus 0.79 with age, minus 0.60 with severity, and minus 0.64 with stress.

16
00:03:12,435 --> 00:03:25,086
The covariates are also correlated with each other, outlined in purple. Severity and stress have a correlation of 0.67. Age and stress have 0.57.

17
00:03:25,536 --> 00:03:37,504
So when stress goes up, age also tends to go up. A plot of satisfaction against stress alone mixes the two effects. We need one model that contains all the covariates.

18
00:03:37,954 --> 00:03:53,997
Lecture 6 asks these questions. First: is mean satisfaction associated with stress, conditional on age and severity? In the model, this is a question about beta 3, the coefficient of stress, outlined in green.

19
00:03:54,447 --> 00:04:07,716
Second: how does mean satisfaction differ between older and younger patients, with the same severity and stress? This is a question about beta 1, the coefficient of age, outlined in purple.

20
00:04:08,166 --> 00:04:17,915
Third: given the age, severity, and stress of a patient, can we predict the satisfaction? This uses the full equation, outlined in blue.

21
00:04:18,365 --> 00:04:27,368
Later, we ask if the association with stress is different for older patients. That needs a new term in the model. Later lectures show it.

22
00:04:27,818 --> 00:04:37,823
Section 2. Question type A asks you to write the model in matrix form, and to give the shape of each part. Method card A has five steps.

23
00:04:38,273 --> 00:04:57,836
Step 1: write the model for one row. Observation i has the response y i, and p covariates, x i 1 to x i p. y i equals beta 0, plus beta 1 x i 1, and so on, plus beta p x i p, plus epsilon i.

24
00:04:58,286 --> 00:05:03,299
Step 2: stack the n responses into the vector y. It is n by 1.

25
00:05:03,749 --> 00:05:18,896
Step 3: the design matrix X. The intercept beta 0 multiplies the number 1 in every row. So the first column of X is a column of ones. A model without an intercept has no column of ones.

26
00:05:19,346 --> 00:05:27,581
The next column holds covariate 1 for all n observations: x 1 1, x 2 1, to x n 1.

27
00:05:28,031 --> 00:05:36,265
We add one column for each covariate, up to covariate p. So X has n rows and p plus 1 columns.

28
00:05:36,715 --> 00:05:51,713
Step 4: beta has one entry for each column of X: beta 0 to beta p. It is p plus 1 by 1. The errors epsilon 1 to epsilon n form the vector epsilon, n by 1.

29
00:05:52,163 --> 00:06:08,269
The errors are independent normal with variance sigma squared. In vector form, epsilon is M V N, with mean 0 and variance sigma squared times the identity. So y is M V N, with mean X beta.

30
00:06:08,719 --> 00:06:12,709
Step 5: check the shapes. The next slide shows this check.

31
00:06:13,159 --> 00:06:30,012
Step 5 of card A: check the shapes. y is n by 1. X is n by p plus 1. beta is p plus 1 by 1. The underlined inner sizes agree, so X beta is n by 1, the same as y and epsilon.

32
00:06:30,462 --> 00:06:45,118
Row i of X times beta gives beta 0, plus beta 1 x i 1, and so on, plus beta p x i p. So row i of the matrix equation is the model for observation i.

33
00:06:45,568 --> 00:06:52,779
The slides assume p plus 1 is less than n. We need more observations than unknown coefficients.

34
00:06:53,229 --> 00:07:12,173
Now a tutorial question of type A. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 2, question 3, has five test regions. x i j is 1 if promotion j is used in region i, and 0 if not. The model has no intercept. Write X and y.

35
00:07:12,623 --> 00:07:28,580
Step 1 of card A: one row. Region 1 uses both promotions, so x 1 1 and x 1 2 are both 1. So 11 equals beta 1 times 1, plus beta 2 times 1, plus epsilon 1.

36
00:07:29,030 --> 00:07:35,771
Step 2: y stacks the five responses: 11, 2, 8, 4, 6. It is 5 by 1.

37
00:07:36,221 --> 00:07:53,480
Step 3: X. The model has no intercept, so X has no column of ones. Column 1 is x i 1: it is 1 where P 1 is used. Regions 1, 2 and 4 use P 1. So column 1 is 1, 1, 0, 1, 0.

38
00:07:53,930 --> 00:08:05,706
Column 2 is x i 2: it is 1 where P 2 is used. Regions 1, 3 and 5 use P 2. So column 2 is 1, 0, 1, 0, 1.

39
00:08:06,156 --> 00:08:14,305
Step 4: beta has one entry for each column of X: beta 1 and beta 2. Epsilon is 5 by 1.

40
00:08:14,755 --> 00:08:32,462
Step 5: check. Row 1 of X times beta is beta 1 plus beta 2, as in step 1. y is 5 by 1, X is 5 by 2, and beta is 2 by 1. The 2 columns are fewer than the 5 rows. This is the answer to part b.

41
00:08:32,912 --> 00:08:42,661
A second type A example: the satisfaction data of Lecture 6. Pause the video and try it. There are 46 patients and 3 covariates.

42
00:08:43,111 --> 00:09:04,509
Step 1 of card A: one row. Patient 1 has satisfaction 48, age 50, severity 51 and stress 2.3. So 48 equals beta 0, plus beta 1 times 50, plus beta 2 times 51, plus beta 3 times 2.3, plus epsilon 1.

43
00:09:04,959 --> 00:09:14,260
Step 2: y stacks the 46 satisfaction values: 48, 57, 66, and so on. It is 46 by 1.

44
00:09:14,710 --> 00:09:25,377
Step 3: the model has an intercept, so column 1 of X is all ones. Then come the columns age, severity and stress. X is 46 by 4.

45
00:09:25,827 --> 00:09:35,661
Step 4: beta has one entry for each column: beta 0 to beta 3. It is 4 by 1. Epsilon is 46 by 1.

46
00:09:36,111 --> 00:09:51,087
Step 5: 46 by 4 times 4 by 1 gives 46 by 1, like y. And p plus 1, which is 4, is less than 46. Lecture 6, page 37, builds this X in R.

47
00:09:51,537 --> 00:10:02,631
Section 3: what the coefficients mean. With one covariate, the mean of y is a line. With two covariates, we need three axes: x 1, x 2, and y.

48
00:10:03,081 --> 00:10:08,606
Each observation is one point in this space. The points here are our own example.

49
00:10:09,056 --> 00:10:23,712
The mean of y is beta 0, plus beta 1 x 1, plus beta 2 x 2. This is a plane, in green. With more covariates it is a flat surface in more dimensions, and the ideas are the same.

50
00:10:24,162 --> 00:10:35,874
The error epsilon i is the vertical distance from the point to the plane, in orange. Points above the plane have a positive error. Points below have a negative error.

51
00:10:36,324 --> 00:10:46,756
Question type B asks you to interpret a coefficient. Lecture 6 says: to interpret a parameter, isolate it. Method card B has four steps.

52
00:10:47,206 --> 00:10:55,291
Step 1: write the mean of y as a function of the covariates: beta 0, plus beta 1 x 1, and so on.

53
00:10:55,741 --> 00:11:14,408
Step 2: for beta 0, set every covariate to 0. Then only beta 0 stays. In the picture, beta 0 is the height of the plane at x 1 equals x 2 equals 0. If 0 is far from the data, beta 0 has no practical meaning.

54
00:11:14,858 --> 00:11:26,463
Step 3: for beta j, increase x j by 1, keep the other covariates fixed, and subtract the two means. In the picture, the plane rises by beta 1.

55
00:11:26,913 --> 00:11:38,007
Step 4: write the answer. It names the one-unit increase in x j, the units, the direction, the mean of y, and the words: holding the other covariates fixed.

56
00:11:38,457 --> 00:11:45,603
Step 1 of card B: write the mean of y i, given the covariates x i 1 to x i p.

57
00:11:46,053 --> 00:11:57,957
Step 2: set every covariate to 0. The marked covariates in line 1 become 0. Each term beta j x i j becomes beta j times 0.

58
00:11:58,407 --> 00:12:10,247
Each of these terms is 0. Only beta 0 stays. In the picture, beta 0 is the height of the plane above the point x 1 equals x 2 equals 0.

59
00:12:10,697 --> 00:12:15,391
So beta 0 is the mean outcome when all covariates are 0.

60
00:12:15,841 --> 00:12:30,625
This may have no meaning. The data can be far from 0. In the satisfaction data, ages go from 22 to 55, so age 0 is far outside. Then beta 0 only places the plane.

61
00:12:31,075 --> 00:12:42,659
Now beta 1, slide 16. Take a point with covariate values x 1, x 2, and so on. The mean outcome is the height of the plane above this point.

62
00:12:43,109 --> 00:12:56,591
Step 3 of card B: increase x 1 by 1, and keep x 2 and all other covariates the same. The new parts are in orange. The mean outcome is the height of the plane at the new point.

63
00:12:57,041 --> 00:13:15,601
Subtract the two means. Beta 0 cancels. The terms with x 2 to x p cancel, because these values did not change. Only beta 1 times x 1 plus 1, minus beta 1 times x 1, stays. In the picture, this is the rise.

64
00:13:16,051 --> 00:13:23,347
Multiply out. The difference is beta 1. The rise is the same at every point, because the plane is flat.

65
00:13:23,797 --> 00:13:36,576
In general, beta j is the difference in mean outcome for a one-unit change in covariate j, holding the other covariates fixed. Always say: holding the other covariates fixed.

66
00:13:37,026 --> 00:13:49,186
Now a type B example with the satisfaction data. Pause the video and try it. The question bar shows the fitted model. Video 13 b shows how to compute these estimates.

67
00:13:49,636 --> 00:14:01,903
Step 1 of card B: the fitted mean is the equation in the question bar. Write the units: satisfaction is a score, age is in years, and severity and stress are scores.

68
00:14:02,353 --> 00:14:23,622
Step 3 for age: beta 1 hat is minus 1.1416. Take two patients with the same severity and stress. One is 10 years older. The fitted mean of the older patient is lower by 10 times 1.1416, which is 11.4. The picture shows this drop.

69
00:14:24,072 --> 00:14:34,888
Step 4, the answer: for each additional year of age, holding severity and stress fixed, the estimated mean satisfaction decreases by 1.14.

70
00:14:35,338 --> 00:14:56,714
Step 3 for stress: beta 3 hat is minus 13.4702. Fix age and severity, and increase stress by 0.5. The line moves down by 0.5 times 13.4702, which is 6.74. The two lines are parallel.

71
00:14:57,164 --> 00:15:09,047
Step 4, the answer: for each one-unit increase in stress, holding age and severity fixed, the estimated mean satisfaction decreases by 13.47.

72
00:15:09,497 --> 00:15:35,033
Step 2 for beta 0 hat, 158.49: it is the estimated mean satisfaction when age, severity and stress are all 0. Ages go from 22 to 55 in the data, severity from 41 to 62, and stress from 1.8 to 2.9. Zero is far outside, so beta 0 hat has no practical meaning.

73
00:15:35,483 --> 00:15:52,635
This slide is optional. It is not on the slides. A simple regression of satisfaction on stress alone gives a slope of minus 37.12. Age and severity go up with stress, so this slope also contains their effects.

74
00:15:53,085 --> 00:16:02,770
The multiple regression slope of stress is minus 13.47, in green. It compares patients with the same age and severity.

75
00:16:03,220 --> 00:16:07,615
So an answer of type B always says which covariates are held fixed.

76
00:16:08,065 --> 00:16:19,307
A type B question from Tutorial 2, question 3 a. Pause the video and try it. The model has no intercept. What does this assume, and why is it reasonable?

77
00:16:19,757 --> 00:16:31,192
Step 1 of card B: write the mean. It is beta 1 x i 1 plus beta 2 x i 2. The grid shows the mean for each setting of the two promotions.

78
00:16:31,642 --> 00:16:47,429
Step 2: set every covariate to 0. With no promotion, x i 1 and x i 2 are both 0. So the mean is beta 1 times 0, plus beta 2 times 0, which is 0. The marked cell shows it.

79
00:16:47,879 --> 00:17:02,449
So the model assumes that the mean change in market share is 0 when neither promotion is used. This is reasonable: y is the change after the promotion, so with no promotion the change should be 0.

80
00:17:02,899 --> 00:17:16,937
To recap. Card A writes the model in matrix form: one row, the vector y, the design matrix X with a column of ones when the model has an intercept, the vector beta, and a check of the shapes.

81
00:17:17,387 --> 00:17:33,749
Card B interprets the coefficients. Beta 0 is the mean outcome when all covariates are 0. Beta j is the difference in mean outcome for a one-unit change in covariate j, holding the other covariates fixed.

82
00:17:34,199 --> 00:17:44,717
Video 13 b derives the least squares estimate, beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, X transpose y, and shows how to compute it.
